// Power-down state machine (SOC.md 2.5; timers-clock 7.2):
//   RUN     --sleep_req rises-->  ENTRY    cpu_run = 0 (the core halts); the CPU clock (cpu_clk: PMCTR) still runs
//   ENTRY   --the ENTRY_PCE-th pce, no Pclk write in flight-->  SLEEP / DSLEEP / STANDBY by STBY and DSLP (now)
//   ENTRY   --wake-->  RESYNC, the CPU clock still running (counted)
//   SLEEP   cpu_run = cpu_clk = 0; enables keep running        DSLEEP  as SLEEP, and dmac_run = 0
//   STANDBY cpu_run = cpu_clk = 0; enables and div stop; stby = 1
//   SLEEP / DSLEEP --wake-->  RESYNC (clock stopped) --RESYNC_PCE-th pce--> RUN
//   STANDBY --wake_stby-->  SETTLE (WDT counts from WTCNT, no flags) --wdt_ovf--> RESYNC --RESYNC_PCE-th pce--> RUN
//   RUN / ENTRY --FRQCR write needing a PLL relock-->  SETTLE (one WDT count per relock) --wdt_ovf (last)--> RUN
//   any     --power-on or manual reset-->  RUN
// * sleep_req is taken on its rising edge (`sleep_q`, sampled in RUN); a one-cycle pulse works the same.  The core
//   presents it as a level (SLEEP issued, EX / MA / S empty, no take under way) that it drops while run = 0 and in
//   the first cycle after run rises (v1_core's `run_q`; SOC.md 2.5, 4.4.3).  That first RUN cycle clears `sleep_q`,
//   so a core still asleep after a spurious wake (the waking request gone during RESYNC, no interrupt taken at the
//   first D cycle) raises sleep_req again one cycle later, a new edge, and re-enters sleep [P].  A woken core's take
//   keeps the level low; a level-sensitive CPG gives the same with this core.  (Only a source that held the level
//   through that first RUN cycle would not be taken again -- v1_cpg_tb's held-level check pins the edge detector with
//   one -- and the core never does that.)  An edge that meets a relocking FRQCR write (the write's settle wins the
//   cycle; also one that lands during ENTRY) is kept in `sleep_pend` and taken when the settle ends [P].  A request
//   pending when SLEEP completes wakes at once (ENTRY, then RESYNC in the next cycle; timers-clock 4.5.2 [P]).
// * ENTRY is the clock-stop handshake (M1b; SOC.md 2.5).  The core halts at the request (cpu_run = 0 from the next
//   cycle), but the CPU clock runs on -- PMCTR counts (cpu_clk, the elapsed event 0x23 of v1_soc) -- until the
//   ENTRY_PCE-th pce edge after the request, and never while a Pclk write is in flight (p4_wpend, v1_p4bus's
//   pclk_wpend: posted and not yet landed at its slave, SOC.md 3.1.5 Landing), so the mode is decided with the STBCR /
//   STBCR2 the program wrote right before its SLEEP (hm 9.5.1) and standby never stops the Pclk bus under a posted
//   write [P].  ENTRY_PCE = 9 (33-36 Icyc after the request by phase) [HW tools/hw/timers-clock/sleepwarm, 64 warm
//   trials with the PMCTR read A right before the TSTR start and the SLEEP 3 instructions later]: PMCTR1L counts
//   50-66 Icyc from A to a handler that reads it first (J; interquartile 58-66) and 66-86 to one that reads TCNT1 first
//   (S; 66-82), against 28 / 52 with the clock stopped at the request; this depth gives 59 / 79, the only one inside
//   both interquartile ranges (8 stages: 55 / 75, 10: 63 / 83); the handler-to-return part is the silicon's in every
//   trial (82 / 60, unchanged).  The console's stop point varies from trial to trial by up to 4 Pcyc (its J / S
//   spreads), which one depth cannot follow; its E sweep (the underflow across the entry) shows the clock still
//   running for an underflow ~30 Icyc after A: a wake in ENTRY is taken with the clock never stopped (below).
// * RESYNC is the wake synchroniser, RESYNC_PCE = 4 Pclk-domain stages: cpu_run rises after the 4th pce edge that
//   follows the cycle the wake is seen, i.e. 14 / 15 / 16 / 17 Icyc for a wake seen in ph 2 / 1 / 0 / 3.  v1_intc's
//   `wake` is its request register `rq`, which moves only after a bce, so it rises in ph 1 or ph 3 only: every on-chip
//   source (TMU, RTC, SCIF, ...; the source changes after a pce) reaches it in ph 1, so the CPG adds 15 Icyc to a TMU
//   wake (tuni -> wake 5 Icyc in v1_intc, -> cpu_run 15 more); an NMI / IRL wake can also land in ph 3 (17 Icyc).
//   tb/v1_clkwake_tb.cpp (make v1-clkwake) checks these numbers with the real v1_intc.  The measured quantity is end
//   to end [HW timers-clock S: 2 Pphi/4 ticks after the reload at the handler's first TCNT1 read in 26 of 32 trials,

// ---- FDIV / FSQRT special cases (v1_fdiv's load stage and the FPU's operand check share them) ------------------
// spec = 1: the result is r / x without the recurrence; e = 1: the FPU error.  a = FRn (dividend / radicand),
// b = FRm (divisor).
//   FSQRT: NaN (V if signalling); +-0 -> +-0; negative nonzero (denormal and -inf included) -> V; DN = 0 positive
//          denormal -> E [HW fpdn]; +inf -> +inf.
//   FDIV : sNaN -> V; qNaN -> qNaN; x / 0: 0 / 0 V, inf / 0 inf (no Z), otherwise Z and inf -- a DN = 0 denormal
//          dividend is a nonzero x, Z beats E [HW fpdn]; then E for a denormal; inf / inf V; inf / x inf; x / inf
//          and 0 / x -> 0 (signs xor).
//   FDIV DR, DN = 0 [HW fpdn part 3]: the high-word NaN; a zero divisor (a low-word-only denormal dividend gives the
//          signed zero with no cause: the high words read 0 / 0, the low word only takes the "invalid" away); the
//          high-word denormals (E); the dividend's low-word NaN; its low-word-only denormal (the signed zero over a
//          low-word-only denormal divisor, else E); the divisor's low-word NaN; inf / inf V; inf / x inf; 0 / x and
//          x / inf 0; a low-word-only denormal divisor last (E).
typedef struct packed { logic spec; logic [63:0] r; fexc_t x; logic e; } dspec_t;
function automatic dspec_t fdiv_spec(input logic [63:0] a, input logic [63:0] b, input logic sq, input logic dbl,
                                     input logic dn0 = 1'b0);
  dspec_t o;
  fu_t    ua, ub;
  logic   s;
  fd_res_t k;
  ua = unpack(a, dbl, dn0); ub = unpack(b, dbl, dn0);
  s  = ua.s ^ ub.s;
  k  = mk(k_qnan(dbl), 1'b0, 1'b0);
  o.spec = 1'b1;
  if (sq) begin
    if (ua.nan)                   k = mk(k_qnan(dbl), ua.snan, 1'b0);
    else if (ua.zero)             k = mk(k_zero(ua.s, dbl), 1'b0, 1'b0);
    else if (ua.s)                k = mk(k_qnan(dbl), 1'b1, 1'b0);          // -norm, -denormal, -inf
    else if (ua.den)              k = mk_e();
    else if (ua.inf)              k = mk(k_inf(1'b0, dbl), 1'b0, 1'b0);
    else                          o.spec = 1'b0;
  end else if (dbl && dn0) begin
    if (ua.snan || ub.snan)       k = mk(k_qnan(dbl), 1'b1, 1'b0);
    else if (ua.hnan || ub.hnan)  k = mk(k_qnan(dbl), 1'b0, 1'b0);
    else if (ub.zero) begin
      if (ua.nan)                 k = mk(k_qnan(dbl), 1'b0, 1'b0);
      else if (ua.zero)           k = mk(k_qnan(dbl), 1'b1, 1'b0);
      else if (ua.lden)           k = mk(k_zero(s, dbl), 1'b0, 1'b0);
      else if (ua.inf)            k = mk(k_inf(s, dbl), 1'b0, 1'b0);
      else                        k = mk(k_inf(s, dbl), 1'b0, 1'b1);

// Units, and the timing each one's structure implies (c = issue cycle of the producer; "D x" = a consumer may issue in
// cycle x; the numbers are the SH7091's, measured by the timings2 sweep):
//   * FE pipe F1 F2 FS (FADD FSUB FMUL FMAC FLOAT FTRC FCMP, FRCHG / FSCHG passes).  Operands are read in D and taken
//     at F1 through a bypass from the integer LS pipe (FP moves are known in EX, FP loads at the end of MA); the FE
//     pipe itself has no bypass: its result reaches readers only through the register file at FS.  So an FE result is
//     read at D c+3 (F1 F2 FS), an FP move's at D c (a follower of the same pair) or later, an FP load's at D c+2.
//     An FP store takes its data at the start of EX, one stage later than D: it may issue at c+2 (the FE pass is in
//     FS when the store is in EX).  STS FPUL,Rn takes FPUL in MA: FTRC -> STS FPUL,Rn at c+1.
//     FCMP's T goes to the CPU at the end of F1 (T readers at D c+2); its flags reach the architectural SR at FS
//     (STC SR at c+3).  The cause bits reach FPSCR one cycle after FS: LDS / STS FPSCR at c+4.
//   * double precision (PR = 1): the op holds F1 (and the array) for L1 passes, then sends the lower word, and one
//     cycle later the upper word, to F2: FADD / FSUB / FMUL DR L1 = 6 (words at FS c+7 / c+8), FLOAT / FCNVSD DR
//     L1 = 2 (c+3 / c+4), FTRC / FCNVDS DR L1 = 2 (FPUL at c+4), FDIV / FSQRT DR L1 = 2 (setup, then the divider).
//     Its operands are read from the register file only (no bypass from the LS pipe: an FP move or load is read at
//     S, c+3).  While the op is in F1 a comparator on its destination pair holds late readers (FP stores, STS FPUL)
//     and, until its last pass, later writers; FE ops and the array wait for F1.  FADD / FSUB / FMUL DR re-read their
//     operands in their first F1 pass, so a writer of a source waits one cycle (D c+2).
//   * divider (F3, v1_fdiv): FDIV / FSQRT single start at F1 with the bypassed operands, DR start in D.  The quotient
//     reaches the register file through a result pass of its own: the recurrence posts a pass request three divider
//     cycles before the quotient is rounded (v1_fdiv `rq`); the request takes the FPU dispatch slot (no FE instruction
//     issues in that cycle: the "steal") in the first cycle the FE pipe is not held, and the pass then runs F1 F2 FS
//     like any FE pass and writes FR at FS (a double's upper word one pass, one cycle, later).  With no hold the pass
//     is granted when it is posted: FS at c+12 / c+11 (single, FS - 3 = the steal at c+9 / c+8) and c+24 / c+23 (DR,
//     then the upper word), v1's numbers.  The next divide may start when the recurrence frees (D c+10 / c+9 /
//     c+23 / c+22: the divider's own `busy` / `last`) and a destination record is free.  Late readers and later
//     writers wait for the destination until the final pass has left F1 (FS - 1); readers until its FS.
//   * vector unit (rtl/fpu_vec: FIPR 1 array pass, FSRRA 3, FSCA 4; FTRV = four FIPR passes): operands from the
//     register file in D; element results at FS c+4 (FIPR), c+6 (FSRRA, FSCA sine), c+7 (FSCA cosine), c+4..c+7
//     (FTRV rows).  The array is busy c+1..c+p (another vector op, a multiply, FRCHG / FSCHG wait: D c+p); each pass
//     then uses F1's adder, so FE arithmetic waits for the last one (D c+p+1).  A later writer of the destination waits
//     until the last FS - 2 (the write order); FTRV locks its destination FV group against LS FP accesses the same way
//     (the compare ignores the bank bit and, for FE writers, covers only DR(4n)).
//   * multiplier: MUL / DMUL / MAC write MACH:MACL at FS c+6 (operand stages, one F2 accumulate, FS); CLRMAC and
//     LDS MACx enter two stages later (FS c+5).  The array stages hold the FE issue port while the FS of a multiply is
//     three or more cycles away (FE at c+4 after MUL, c+3 after CLRMAC); STS MACx,Rn samples MACH / MACL in its MA
//     stage (c+4 / c+3), STS.L MACx in EX (c+5 / c+4).  Multiplies flow in order through the same stages and never
//     wait for each other.
//   * FPSCR mode: FRCHG / FSCHG flip the decoder's FR / SZ at issue (a follower of the same pair cannot use it);
//     LDS FPSCR loads it at S, so every FPU-decoded instruction waits until then (D c+4).  The FPU decoder claims the
//     whole F opcode group before it knows the mode, so a code that is illegal in the old mode waits too.

k_ld   = ocq.kind == DK_LD || ocq.kind == DK_RMW_RD;
k_st   = ocq.kind == DK_ST || ocq.kind == DK_RMW_WR;
k_mv   = ocq.kind == DK_MOVCA;
k_tr   = ocq.kind == DK_TAS_RD;
k_tw   = ocq.kind == DK_TAS_WR;
k_pf   = ocq.kind == DK_PREF;
k_op   = ocq.kind == DK_OCBP;
k_ow   = ocq.kind == DK_OCBWB;
k_pg   = ocq.kind == DK_TAS_PURGE;
is_ram = ocq.x.rg == RG_OCRAM;
cach   = ocq.x.c && ccr.oce && !is_ram;
wtp    = ocq.x.wt;
cidx   = ccr.oix ? {ocq.va[25], ocq.va[12:5]} : ocq.va[13:5];
if (ccr.ora) cidx[7] = 1'b0;
ridx   = {ccr.oix ? ocq.va[25] : ocq.va[13], 1'b1, ocq.va[11:5]};
aidx   = is_ram ? ridx : cidx;
q      = ocq.va[4:3];
a      = ocq.va[2:0];
sbe    = k_mv ? 8'h0F : ocq.be;
hit    = cach && vld[cidx] && tag[cidx] == ocq.x.pa[28:10];
// (once a queued fill has claimed f's entry, f's line is gone for every younger access: it misses, as the reference
// model has it replaced by the younger miss -- M3, the same-entry fq below)
lm_f   = f.v && !f.kill && !(fq.v && fq.idx == f.idx) && f.idx == cidx && f.pa == ocq.x.pa[28:5];
lm     = cach && (lm_f || (fq.v && !fq.kill && fq.idx == cidx && fq.pa == ocq.x.pa[28:5]));
// the V-clear comparator: this entry's V / U are written this cycle (an OCBI / OCBP / purge hit acked last
// cycle): the compare waits one cycle [HW: the six OCBI records]
vch    = cach && vc_v && vc_idx == cidx;
vdirty = vld[cidx] && dty[cidx];
port   = !fw_v;                              // the data RAM's port is free (no fill write this cycle)
// an OC RAM access waits while a fill still targets its entry (claimed before CCR.ORA was set)
ram_ok = port && !(f.v && f.idx == ridx) && !(fq.v && fq.idx == ridx);
line_q = dat[aidx][q];
// needs the bus (decided in m, latched for the rest of the access)
if (is_ram || k_pg)            nb0 = 1'b0;
else if (k_tr || k_tw)         nb0 = 1'b1;
else if (k_st || k_mv)         nb0 = !cach || wtp || (!hit && !lm);
else                           nb0 = !cach || (!hit && !lm);       // LD, PREF, OCBI, OCBP, OCBWB
nb     = wc == 2'd0 ? nb0 : nb_q;
ph_ok  = !nb || wc != 2'd0;                  // may allocate: from m + 1 on the miss path
ack_ok = !nb || wc == 2'd2;                  // may ack: from m + 2 on the miss path

//   * Write front end (W_FE = 2 register stages, per master).  A CPU write is acked no earlier than its third cycle,
//     its MA3 (store data joins the address two stages behind it).  On the Iclk path a write (either master's) strobes
//     there and acks in its capture cycle (BARA write 3 Icyc; PMCR write lands at the end of MA3, App. A.3).  A posted
//     (the CPU's Bclk / Pclk) write acks there too, or when the path accepts it if that is later (posted write 3 Icyc,
//     App. A.4: A ; W ; B = 6 on either path).  A DMAC write on a Bclk / Pclk path does not wait for it (below).
//   * Iclk read.  Strobed combinationally in the cycle the request first appears (MA1: the PMCTR sample point,
//     App. A.3), then I_D2 = 2 return stages: ack in MA3, 3 Icyc (CCR / PMCTR / BARA / EXPEVT).  An array slave
//     stalls its first strobe (synchronous array RAM): 4 Icyc (App. A.5); PTEH's one write stall: 4 Icyc.
//   * Bclk / Pclk request register + synchroniser (D1 = 1).  Any access, read or write, enters the path's slot at the
//     end of the first cycle it is presented with the slot free (the CPU's MA1 when the path is idle); the strobe is
//     the first bce / pce cycle in which that register is visible, i.e. 1..2 (Bclk) or 1..4 (Pclk) cycles later by
//     phase.  So a posted write can be strobed before its own ack (in MA2), and a TCNT read samples 3..6 Icyc after
//     the load issues ("about one Pcyc", timers-clock 4.1 J / S).
//   * Read-return synchroniser (B_D2 = 6, P_D2 = 11 stages after the last transfer).  Occupancy = phase + D1 + D2:
//       Bclk read   8 back to back (4 Bcyc), 8..9 isolated by phase         (BCR1 PDTRA RTCNT DMAOR SAR1: 8 / 8)
//       Pclk read  16 back to back (4 Pcyc), 13..16 isolated by phase       (probe rd16 B - A 16 19 18 17)
//       Pclk read32 20 back to back (5 Pcyc), 17..20 isolated              (probe rd32 20 23 22 21; ldTMU loop:
//                   the second 16-bit transfer adds one Pcyc               occupancy locks at 18, event 0x25 = 17)
//     8 reads + a 2-cycle loop tail (core-integration 6.1): Bclk 66 (the tail is not hidden), Pclk 128 and TCNT 160
//     (hidden in the synchroniser slack).
//   * Posted-write entry release (WREL = 2).  The path-side bus controller has taken the whole datum at the first
//     path clock edge after the strobe (the next pce / bce); that "taken" flag is synchronised back through WREL
//     Iclk stages, and the entry is free 3 cycles after that edge: Pclk strobe + 7, Bclk strobe + 5, for a byte,
//     word or longword alike.  The next access on the path enters the slot then.
//   * Bus cycle (Bclk / Pclk).  A bus cycle lasts its transfers + one recovery clock: a strobe loads a path-clock
//     counter with the number of transfers (a Pclk longword 2, anything else 1), which counts down on the following
//     path clock edges; the next strobe waits for it to be 0.  So after a Pclk word write the path's next strobe is
//     2 Pcyc later, after a longword write 3 Pcyc ("drains in 2 / 3 Pcyc", timers-clock 3.0).  (After a read the
//     slot is held until the ack, far beyond the recovery clock.)
//     With the entry release, the posted writes give:
//       Pclk write  8 / 12 back to back (IPRB / TCOR1: 8 / 12), 3 isolated; 8 writes + the loop tail: 64 / 96
//                   16 x mov.w 124 / 127 / 126 / 125 and 16 x mov.l 180 / 183 / 182 / 181 by phase (probe w16x16,
//                   w32x16; tc F 124 / 181); w32 ; w16 ; w16 24 .. 27 but w16 ; w32 ; w16 20 .. 23 (the entry is
//                   free at strobe + 7 whatever the size; the longword's recovery holds the next strobe)
//       Pclk write -> read (the same or another module) 24 / 27 / 26 / 25 (probe w16_r16, w16_r16o; timers-clock T:
//                   26), 8 such pairs 192 .. 195 (tc F 192); longword write -> read 28 .. 31
//       Bclk write  6 back to back (RTCOR keyed, SAR1: 6), 3 isolated
//     An access on another path never waits for an entry (App. A.4: the Iclk read after an IPRA write costs 3).
//   * pclk_wpend (-> v1_cpg): a write is in flight on the Pclk path, from its acceptance to its landing; the CPG takes
//     a sleep request only without one (SOC.md 2.5; the standby entry of hm 9.5.1 needs the STBCR write landed).

