// C3: the BIU is combinational for the requester it picks -- the winner's bq goes to cpu_bq, cpu_bs.ready to the
// winner, cpu_bwd from the requester whose write is on the bus -- so a request raised in m + 1 can be `ready` in m + 1
// (a bce cycle) or m + 2; its only state is the order:
//   * an age matrix: who raised its request first (a request is new in the cycle bq.v rises, or the cycle after its
//     ready if it stays up); ties in one cycle: OC fill, OC uncached read, WTB, WBB, SQ, IC fill, IC uncached word
//     [P: OC before IC, caches-sq 9.7; a fill before its own write-back, measured];
//   * eligibility, as measured: the WTB drains before any read (a read is not presented while the WTB asks)
//     [HW caches-sq 3.2.3, bsc-memmap 4.7: store -> load 26 / 28 per pair]; a read of the line waiting in the WBB waits
//     for the WBB [caches-sq 3.2.2, inferred]; after a locked read (TAS.B) only the WTB, with the pair's write, until
//     that write is accepted (SOC.md 3.3: no hand-over inside a TAS pair);
//   * the oldest eligible request is presented; the bus is in order: a younger one never overtakes the presented one,
//     except the WTB, which by the rule above displaces a presented read (the stub's "WTB overtakes an older read").
//     So cpu_bq may change before `ready`; v1_bsc takes no action on its CPU port except in the cycle it pulses ready.
//   * an owner queue (2 entries: v1_bsc carries at most a read's tail plus one more access) routes the strobes back:
//     rd_v / done in order -- the head gets them and leaves at its `done` (a read's rd_last) --, and wd_req / wd_beat
//     (and cpu_bwd) to the oldest write in the queue: v1_bsc accepts a write while a line read's tail is still being
//     delivered (its write-after-line rule, M3), so the write pulls its data while the line's beats go to the fill.
// Resets (SOC.md 2.3): a power-on reset clears everything; a manual reset keeps the owner queue (a started bus
// transaction completes) and ends a CPU bus lock: the TAS pair's lock (`lk`) is dropped -- the core restarts, so the
// pair's write never comes; v1_bsc_arb drops its side too --, and a locked read still presented across the reset
// (`mr`: its bq.v was up in a rst.man cycle and has not been readied since) is served unlocked, so it neither takes
// the TAS lock here nor makes v1_bsc hold the bus for a write that will not come.  Nothing is accepted during rst.man
// (v1_bsc), so every such read reaches the BSC after the reset.  The DMAC's lock is not the BIU's (v1_bsc's own port):
// it is kept.  The requesters drop their queued requests themselves; a request they still present is served.
// Simulation-only handshake checks (`ifndef SYNTHESIS`): bq.v withdrawn or bq.r changed before ready, a strobe with no
// owner -- a "%Error-BIU" line each.
module v1_biu import v1_soc_pkg::*, v1_bsc_pkg::*; (
  input  logic   clk,
  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */          // rst.pin / cpu, ce: the SOC.md 3.8 port convention (Iclk logic)
  input  rst_t   rst,
  input  clken_t ce,
  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */
  input  bq_t    rq  [BIU_N],
  output bs_t    rs  [BIU_N],
  input  bwd_t   rwd [BIU_N],
  output bq_t    cpu_bq,
  input  bs_t    cpu_bs,
  output bwd_t   cpu_bwd
);
  // the tie order: rank of each requester (0 first)

// The requester side (SOC.md 3.3.2): every strobe is a one-Icyc pulse in a bce cycle.
//   * wd_req: a write's beats are pulled one per Bcyc from b1 (a 32-byte write 4, else 1); bwd is captured at the end
//     of the Icyc after wd_req into one of two write-data buffers (ping-pong per write: a write may still be on the bus
//     while the next one pulls).
//   * done of a write: the later of "its last beat captured" and "its (last) WRIT / address cycle issued": the posted
//     write then retires from its requester.  Single SDRAM write b4, a burst b5, a quadword (two longword cycles) b9:
//     with the bus rule, stores stream at 10, bursts at 12, 64-bit stores at 20 Icyc [HW bsc-memmap 4.7].
//   * rd_v / rd_beat / rd_data / rd_last: a read beat delivered in the Bcyc it is on the bus (the board answers
//     combinationally, SOC.md 3.3.2); a B / W / L read returns its lanes only (others 0); a line in wrap order from the
//     critical quadword; done = rd_last.  Narrow buses and the Q's two halves are assembled here (`asm`).
//   * a CPU BZ_Q is split into two longword accesses (SOC.md 3.3.2), the second entering the front end once the first
//     is done; the requester sees one ready, one rd_v with the whole quadword / one wd_req and one done -- 30 Icyc per
//     uncached 64-bit read, 20 per 64-bit store [HW bsc-memmap 4.7].  A DMAC 8-byte unit is not split: it is one
//     access of size Q -- one 64-bit data cycle on a 64-bit bus (the DC), so it costs what every other unit size costs
//     [HW dmac 3.13 obs. 1: 46.3 Icyc per dual unit at 1-32 bytes]; on a narrower bus it is that bus's width split of
//     one access (two data cycles of one SDRAM / MPX cycle on a 32-bit bus, the pieces of a basic interface) [P].
// DMAW records (SOC.md 6.3): every write beat of the DMAC port (dual: its data; single-address device -> memory: the
// board's data at the beat [P]); isn = 0 (the soc testbench stamps the next ISN [P]).
module v1_bsc import v1_soc_pkg::*, v1_bsc_pkg::*; #(
  parameter int LOCK_HOLD = 16                   // TAS.B bus hold, Bcyc from the locked read's accept (v1_bsc_arb)
) (
  input  logic                          clk,
  input  rst_t                          rst,
  input  clken_t                        ce,
  input  p4s_req_t                      pq,
  output p4s_rsp_t                      pr,
  input  p4s_req_t                      pq_sdmr,
  output p4s_rsp_t                      pr_sdmr,
  input  logic                          bpost_bsc,
  input  bq_t                           cpu_bq,
  output bs_t                           cpu_bs,
  input  bwd_t                          cpu_bwd,
  input  bq_t                           dma_bq,
  output bs_t                           dma_bs,
  input  bwd_t                          dma_bwd,
  output xb_out_t                       xb_o,
  input  xb_in_t                        xb_i,
  input  logic                          breq_n,
  output logic                          back_n,
  input  logic                          ddt_bus_req,

//   * bpost_bsc (SOC.md 3.1.5): no new CPU / DMAC access while a posted BSC / SDMR register write is pending.
//   * (M6) the CPU's turn: when a CPU access waits while the DMAC holds no bus session (dma_rel: v1_dmac's engine in
//     REL, or idle with nothing to commit -- a break in its requests), the next accept is the CPU's (cpu_turn): a DMAC
//     request raised after that waits until the CPU's access is accepted, also while the bus is still busy with the
//     DMAC's last access (a single-address burst the next one would overlap, hm table 13.16) and while the CPU's access
//     waits out WCR1's idle cycles (an area-0 read after the DMAC's area-3 burst: A3 -> A0 needs one).  Without it a
//     DMAC stream whose requests come a Bcyc or two apart (Holly's CH2-DMA requests, paced by its input FIFO) took
//     every accept and starved an MPX read for the whole transfer: the chip polled ISTNRM once in 64 KB where [HW dmac1
//     S13] the console's loop kept polling (M6's first dmac1 run, "ISTNRM19 at 83 cyc"); hm 14.3.4 and figure 14.11's
//     note: "the bus is passed to the CPU during a break in requests"; [HW dmac2 T5]: one CPU transaction between
//     cycle-steal units.  A burst session never releases (dma_rel = 0), so a burst still keeps the bus from the CPU
//     [HW dmac2 T4]; a lock (TAS.B, a DMAC dual pair) is not broken (the turn is taken only with no lock).
//   * (M6-fix) a burst session between the DMAC's own accesses (dma_ses = v1_dmac.bus_ses: a burst-mode channel holds
//     the bus): the DMAC wants the bus as if it had a request up, so a CPU access is not accepted in those Bcyc either
//     (the dual unit that resumes a burst after a higher-priority single unit -- a Holly DTR unit, a CH2-DMA unit
//     under round robin -- waits for that unit's beats with bq.v low).  Only the CPU's turn (taken at a break before
//     the session began) and the CPU's own locked pair (its write after a TAS.B read accepted in the Bcyc the burst's
//     first unit was committed) still go.  hm 14.3.4: "the bus is not released to the CPU until channel 1 transfer
//     ends"; before, 21 CPU reads completed inside a 1,024-unit burst preempted by a 1 KB PVR-DMA and 1,002 inside
//     one alternating with a 64 KB CH2-DMA (M6-verify ddtmix G / H), now none.
// Refresh / SDMR commands and the external requests do not use the front end: granted when the front end is empty and
// this Bcyc is the bus's last busy one (or it is idle), so a refresh costs a read stream the same 24 Icyc whatever the
// phase [HW bsc-memmap 6.2].  A pending refresh blocks new accepts.
// Bus release (BREQ#, master mode, BREQEN): after the current access, PALL if a row is open, then BACK# low; while
// released a refresh request negates BACK# to ask for the bus back; BREQ# high for 2 Bcyc gives it back [P].  BREQ#
// asserted again inside those 2 Bcyc (hm 13.3.12 asks for at least 2 cycles of negation): the bus is taken back at
// once and released again by the ordinary rule, so it is never left with no owner [P].
// DDT (dmac 3.9, SOC.md 3.3.3): ddt_bus_req is granted with a one-Bcyc ddt_bavl pulse when the bus is free; the data
// bus is then kept for the DTR two Bcyc later [P].
module v1_bsc_arb import v1_soc_pkg::*, v1_bsc_pkg::*; #(
  parameter int LOCK_HOLD = 16
) (
  input  logic              clk,
  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */          // rst.pin / cpu: pin implies por; a CPU-only reset holds the BSC
  input  rst_t              rst,
  input  clken_t            ce,
  input  bregs_t            regs,
  input  bq_t               cpu_bq,
  input  bq_t               dma_bq,
  input  bx_t               q2_x,                // a split Q's second half (addr, we, port, master)

wire         pfz = (lt_frz && !kill_ma) || oc_coll || (lreq.v && lrsp.frz);

// ============================================================================================================
// D stage: the two decode slots
dec_t   d [2];
ictl_t  c [2];
logic   s_v [2];
tp_decode u_dec0 (.op(q_op[0]), .md(md_dec), .d(d[0]));
tp_decode u_dec1 (.op(q_op[1]), .md(md_dec), .d(d[1]));
assign c[0] = v1_ictl_op(d[0].form, q_op[0]);
assign c[1] = v1_ictl_op(d[1].form, q_op[1]);
// the claim window (SOC.md 4.3.4 item 2): v1_fetch's pre-decode of the queue from q0 on, continued by the word it
// reads in this cycle -- what "already fetched at t" means [HW c1i, c1j, c1b j0: see v1_fetch]; up to 3 behind the
// excepting instruction [HW c1i: a load 2 and 3 places behind claims].  A poisoned entry has no opcode (v1_fetch
// gives it op = 0, a NOP to the decoder): no claim, no branch, no tags beyond the UBC's (a break outranks the fault).
// A follower-candidate take's claimants (positions 2, 3) do not claim when they read a register the leader issuing
// in the take cycle writes (`q_dep`) [P, the one console case that tells: c1b d0 (A = a PMCTR read at a BRA target,
// X = H'FFFD its follower, then a store of A's result, fetched in the take cycle: + 0) against j0 (the same at a JSR
// target with a PC-relative load, independent: + 2); a store of a result loaded one cycle before t claims (c1i st,
// c1b d2)].
// The fetch poison of SOC.md 4.1 item 2 rides the entry (q_fxe): an odd PC or MD = 0 at or above 0x80000000 (v1_fetch,
// with the MD the entry was fetched with: SOC.md 4.1 item 9, the RTE slot's old MD included), and v1_mmu's region /
// ITLB faults; it is taken at D (SOC.md 4.3.2 priority: after the pre-execution break) with its own code / vector.
logic   q2_mem, q3_mem;
dec_t   dw [2];                               // positions 2 / 3 of the claim window, decoded for q_dep
logic   q_dep [2];                            // ... reads a register the leader issuing this cycle writes
tp_decode u_decw2 (.op(f_w_op2), .md(md_dec), .d(dw[0]));
tp_decode u_decw3 (.op(f_w_op3), .md(md_dec), .d(dw[1]));
logic   q_br [3];                             // q0 - q2 is a branch: the entries after it (after its slot) are not
                                              // the program order [P]
logic   q_fx [2];                             // q0 / q1 is poisoned
logic   qm [2];                               // q0 / q1 is a memory instruction (the claim)
assign q_fx[0] = q_v[0] && q_fxe[0] != FX_NONE;
assign q_fx[1] = q_v[1] && q_fxe[1] != FX_NONE;
assign qm[0] = f_w_mem[0];
assign qm[1] = f_w_mem[1];
assign q2_mem = f_w_mem[2];
assign q3_mem = f_w_mem[3];
assign q_br[0] = f_w_br[0];
assign q_br[1] = f_w_br[1];

assign kill_ma = rst_c || stake_c;           // SOC.md 3.2.2 rule 4: reset, and the channel-B data break at S

// ---- issue
wire busy = sq_on || frz;
wire ent_blk = ent_act || exc_sv || rst_wait;
wire lead = s_v[0] && !busy && !sleep_hit && !ent_blk;
wire cpu0 = |cwhy[0];
wire iss0_raw = lead && !cpu0 && !fhzc[0];
wire iss1_raw = iss0_raw && s_v[1] && pair_ok && !dep1 && !(|cwhy[1]) && !fhzc[1];
assign s_v[0] = q_v[0];
assign s_v[1] = q_v[1];
// the leader's issue-cycle count: the sequencer's step counter holds D for k cycles.  SLEEP is CO with k = 4 in
// v1_pkg's v1_ictl_op (SOC.md 4.4.3, 1.3.1; hm Table 8.3; exceptions-intc 3.15), like every multi-cycle CO form
wire [2:0] k0 = c[0].k;
// ---- interrupt acceptance (SOC.md 4.4.1, C14): the boundary is a D cycle whose leader could be interrupted -- valid
// (asleep: the instruction after SLEEP, fetched or not), not a delay slot (never between a delayed branch, RTE
// included, and its slot [HW excint C1h / PB / PS: 72 of 72]), no sequence running (a multi-micro-op instruction is
// never split), no hold (pfz, mah, run = 0), no entry in progress.  irq.req & irq.nmi is acceptable as the INTC
// presents it (BL / NMIB applied there); an ordinary request needs SR.BL = 0 (with the S write-through: the leader
// after an LDC SR / RTE that clears BL is taken at once [HW: k = 0, 24 of 24 + 8 of 8]), or asleep (BL ignored [M
// 9.3.2]).  Program order: an older MA / S take wins (pk_v); the interrupt wins over the leader's own D-stage fault
// or D-decided FPU trap [HW excint2 PF: SPC = the H'FFFD, which traps after the RTE].
// With the window empty while the fetch waits -- an I-miss not yet answered for a cycle, or a redirect waiting for an
// outstanding uncached word / ITLB refill -- the next instruction (v1_fetch's fpc) is a boundary too: "the interrupt
// is taken while D starves on an I-miss, with SPC = the missing instruction" [HW core-integration 3.7, cold code]
// (the stall is the registered `f_starve_q`, so the take never feeds back into this cycle's request)
wire f_starve = !q_v[0] && (f_busy_wait || f_starve_q);
wire ibound = (q_v[0] || sleep_issued || f_starve) && !slot0_q && !sq_on && !frz && !ent_blk;
wire itake  = !pk_v && ibound && irq.req && (irq.nmi || !sr_rd[28] || sleep_issued);
wire dstop  = pk_v || itake;                 // no D-stage take and no issue this cycle
// D-stage takes (SOC.md 4.3.2): the leader in the first cycle it would issue (no data comparators [P]); the
// follower candidate in the cycle its leader issues (a one-cycle leader that is not a taken BT / BF, and q1 not a
// branch target: the instruction that would issue next, whatever its pairing class -- `d0`); a D-decided FPU trap
// in its issue cycle, through the issue gate.
wire fc1    = s_v[1] && k0 == 3'd1 && !q_tgt[1] && !(tk0 && !c[0].br_dly);
// An H'Fxxx code is decoded in the FPU's mode (FR / SZ / PR): while an LDS FPSCR is on its way to S that mode is not
// known (v1_fpu's fwhy[2], the decoder lock that holds every F-group opcode), so an H'Fxxx the stale mode leaves
// undecoded is not taken as illegal / slot illegal until the new mode is in (it may decode then).  H'FFFD is illegal
// in every mode.  Likewise the follower candidate of a leader that changes the mode (LDS FPSCR, FRCHG, FSCHG: the
// pair v1_fpu refuses, fdep).  [P, WI-CORE-F: program order, tb/sh4_iss.h; the D take applies no comparators,

// Interrupt levels (spec 3.7, [HW dmac1 S9 / dmac2 T2]): DMTEn = TE & IE (a level, independent of DE), DMAE = AE.
// They are masked by `mstp` (STBCR.MSTP4) alone: a module-stopped DMAC drives them 0 (SOC.md 2.4, 3.4).  Deep sleep
// and standby stop `run` but keep the levels -- they are the TE / IE / AE flop outputs, which hold without the Bclk --
// so a DMTE pending at deep-sleep entry still reaches the INTC and wakes the CPU (SOC.md 2.4, I088).
module v1_dmac import v1_soc_pkg::*, v1_dmac_pkg::*; (
  input  logic      clk,
  input  rst_t      rst,
  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */          // ce.pce: every DMAC flop is Bclk
  input  clken_t    ce,
  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */
  input  logic      run,                       // dmac_run: 0 with MSTP4, in deep sleep and in standby
  input  logic      stby,
  input  logic      mstp,                      // STBCR.MSTP4: DMTE / DMAE driven 0 (SOC.md 2.4)
  input  p4s_req_t  pq,
  output p4s_rsp_t  pr,
  output p4m_req_t  p4q,
  input  p4m_rsp_t  p4r,
  output bq_t       bq,
  input  bs_t       bs,
  output bwd_t      bwd,
  input  dreq_t     dreq,
  output logic      dack_ic,
  input  logic      dbreq_n, tr_n,
  output logic      bavl_n, tdack_n,
  output logic [1:0] ddt_id,
  input  ddt_tl_t   ddt_tl,
  input  logic [63:0] ddt_d,                   // D63:0 (the external data bus): the DTR in the DDT DTR cycle (M6)
  output logic      ddt_bus_req,
  input  logic      ddt_bavl,
  output logic      bus_rel,                   // (M6) no bus session (v1_dmac_xfer): the BSC's CPU turn (SOC.md 3.3.2)
  output logic      bus_ses,                   // (M6-fix) a burst session holds the bus (v1_dmac_xfer): no CPU accept
  input  logic      nmi_evt,
  output logic [3:0] dmte,
  output logic      dmae
);
  // ---- registers
  logic [3:0][31:0] sar, dar, chcr;
  logic [3:0][23:0] tcr;
  logic             ddt, dme, ae, nmif, halt_now;
  logic [1:0]       prm;

  if (!c[5]) f[6:2] = f[6:2] | c[4:0];                  // (an E trap sets no flag)
  if (head) begin
    if (!sv || cz_yeq(t, st)) begin st = t; sc = c; sv = 1'b1; end
  end else if (sv && st == t) sc[4:0] = sc[4:0] | c[4:0];
endtask

// double-precision sequencer
// [0] the operation in its F1 passes, [1] the previous one whose result words are still in F2 / FS when the next
// double issues (a double issues once F1 is free, at age L1 of the previous one; that one retires at L1 + 2)
logic        db_on  [2];
logic [4:0]  db_age [2];              // cycles since issue (1 = first F1 pass)
logic [2:0]  db_l1  [2];
logic [31:0] db_dst [2], db_src [2];  // destination pair / source words
logic        db_fpul[2];              // result goes to FPUL
logic [63:0] db_res [2];
fexc_t       db_x   [2];
logic        db_e   [2];              // E (DN = 0 denormal operand) of the operand check
logic        db_div [2];              // FDIV / FSQRT DR: the divider writes the result
logic [4:0]  db_cz  [2];
logic [7:0]  db_isn [2];
logic        db_lane[2];
// divider: two destination records (the operation in the recurrence and the previous one, whose result passes may
// still be pending or in flight), fixed slots, dv_old = the older when both are on
logic        dv_on  [2];
logic        dv_dbl [2];
logic [31:0] dv_dst [2];
logic [4:0]  dv_cz  [2];
logic [7:0]  dv_isn [2];
logic        dv_st  [2];              // the operation has started in the recurrence (single: at F1; DR: at D)
logic        dv_pst [2];              // its result pass request is posted (v1_fdiv rq)
logic        dv_cap [2];              // its result is captured (v1_fdiv done)
logic [63:0] dv_res [2];
fexc_t       dv_xr  [2];
logic        dv_te  [2];              // a trap pass (destination 0) whose operand check is E: its cause is E alone
                                      // (single: set at the start in F1; DR: at D)
logic [2:0]  ps_lo  [2], ps_hi [2];   // result pass of the lower / single (upper) word: 0 not granted, 1 F1, 2 F2,
                                      // 3 FS (written this cycle), 4 written
logic        dv_old;
logic        dv_start, dv_sqrt, dv_sdbl, dv_busy, dv_last, dv_rq, dv_done;
logic [7:0]  dv_sisn;

  input  rst_t        rst,
  input  p4s_req_t    pq_reg,
  output p4s_rsp_t    pr_reg,
  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */          // pq_arr.dma / off[27:24] / off bits outside the entry fields
  input  p4s_req_t    pq_arr,
  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */
  output p4s_rsp_t    pr_arr,
  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */          // ifq.spec: v1_icache's (no line fill for an abandoned request)
  input  ireq_t       ifq,
  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */
  output logic        if_hit,
  output logic [28:0] if_pa,
  output logic        if_c,
  output fx_e         if_fx,
  output logic        if_vec400, if_stall,
  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */          // dex.size / dex.arch: alignment is the core's, URC is ma_commit
  input  dex_t        dex,
  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */
  output dxlat_t      dxl,
  input  logic        ma_commit, ldtlb,
  input  excw_t       excw,
  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */          // ccr: ICE and the index modes are the caches'
  input  ccr_t        ccr,
  /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */
  input  logic        xl_req, xl_itlb,
  input  logic [31:0] xl_va,
  output logic        xl_hit, xl_mhit, xl_stall,
  output logic [28:0] xl_pa,
  output logic        arr_mhit,
  output logic        at, sqmd,
  output logic [7:0]  asid,
  output commit_rec_t [CT_MMU_N-1:0] tap,
  output pmc_ev_t     pmc_ev
);
  // ==================================================================================================================
  // registers
  logic [21:0] pteh_vpn;
  logic [7:0]  pteh_asid;
  /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */          // PTEL's reserved bits (stored as 0); URB is v1_mmu_regs' (URC step)
  logic [31:0] ptel;

// Planted bugs for the WI-SOC testbench (make v1-soc-mutants; each must be caught): V1_SOC_MUT_MD_LATCH (md latched at
// the end of rst.pin), V1_SOC_MUT_UBC_SEQ / V1_SOC_MUT_UBC_PREARM (the UBC wires tied 0), V1_SOC_MUT_DMAC_MSTP (the
// DMAC's mstp tied 0), V1_SOC_MUT_MA_COMMIT (ma_commit unqualified), V1_SOC_MUT_XL_OR (only the IC's xl request);
// and three that break a protocol rule tb/soc_tb_top.sv's monitors check: V1_SOC_MUT_MA_ACK (the core misses one MA ack
// in eight: the unit sees its request again), V1_SOC_MUT_NULL_SLAVE (the IDEAL_MEM null array slaves answer hit = 1
// unstrobed), V1_SOC_MUT_XL_IDLE (the IDEAL_MEM tie-off of the IC's xl_itlb is 1); V1_SOC_MUT_WPEND (the bridge's
// pclk_wpend not wired to the CPG: STBY <- 1 ; SLEEP sleeps instead of entering standby, selftest `standby`);
// V1_SOC_MUT_IRQ_ORDER (irq_src.tuni0 / tuni1 swapped: equal-level TMU interrupts in the wrong order, which the lockstep
// checker cannot see -- it takes the code from the RTL -- but tb/bm/soc-adv prio does; M1a-verify F5);
// V1_SOC_MUT_CPU_CLK (the elapsed event 0x23 follows cpu_run instead of the CPU clock: the clock-stop handshake after
// a SLEEP goes uncounted, tb/bm/timers-clock sleepwarm W J 28 against the console's 50-66; M1b);
// V1_SOC_MUT_IRL_ORDER (the IRL pins reach v1_intc in reversed bit order: Holly's levels 4 / 2 -- IRL 1011 / 1101 --
// swap, level 6 -- 1001 -- does not; selftest `hollyirl`, M4).
module v1_soc import v1_soc_pkg::*, v1_bsc_pkg::*; #(
  parameter bit IDEAL_MEM = 1'b0,                // 1: v1_ideal_mem instead of IC / OC / SQ / BIU and the BSC's CPU
                                                 // port (M1a, M1b, M2)
  parameter bit TRANSLATE = 1'b1                 // v1_mmu's; 0: identity translation, no TLBs (M1a, M1b, M3)
) (
  input  logic        clk,                       // Iclk
  input  logic        reset_n,                   // RESET; the kind is SCK2's level while it is low (SOC.md 2.2)
  input  logic [8:0]  md,                        // MD8-0 straps: straight to v1_cpg / v1_bsc, which load them during
                                                 // rst.pin (SOC.md 2.2; md[k] = the value the strap loads, DC 0x065)
  // external bus: the fields of xb_out_t / xb_in_t as scalar ports (with IDEAL_MEM = 1 only the BSC's own cycles use
  // it: refresh, the SDRAM mode set, the DMAC)
  output logic        xb_start,
  output logic [2:0]  xb_area,
